- `Review-Base` where relevant;
- `Verdict`: `CLEAN`, `NOT_CLEAN`, or `CLEAN_WITH_WAIVERS`;
- P0/P1/P2 blockers;
- attribution (`introduced_by_candidate`, `pre_existing`, `newly_discovered`, or equivalent) **separately from CLEAN blocking status**;
- exact source paths/functions;
- reproduction/authority chain;
- execution evidence;
- required remediation boundary;
- reviewer/date;
- relation to prior findings;
- whether the ledger may now be closed.

A record for SHA A does not automatically review later SHA B. Later commits may preserve the fix, but that is a hypothesis until the next independent review reconstructs the current path.

## 12.3 Review discovery rule for the implementation model

At bootstrap and after every push:

1. fresh-fetch implementation branch;
2. fresh-fetch `review/remediation`;
3. fresh-fetch ledger;
4. find review records targeting any candidate defect SHA that has not yet been remediated/closed;
5. prefer the newest authoritative independent review for the same exact checkpoint;
6. detect newly published CLEAN records that unlock dependencies;
7. do not treat implementation-model notes as independent review;
8. do not infer `CLEAN` from absence of a review file.

## 12.4 Review branch is evidence transport, not implementation authority

A review record may require a change, classify severity, or establish CLEAN, but production semantics remain on `checkpoint/pre-baseline-review`. Do not merge review-only commits into the implementation branch merely to make the finding visible. The implementation agent responds by creating an additive semantic fix commit on the implementation branch.

---

# 13. Closure ledger protocol — record every independently finished defect

The user's desired historical record is implemented here: after a defect is independently CLEAN, seal the result with a separate metadata/evidence closure commit.

## 13.1 Who may close

The implementation model must not close its own defect merely because tests/self-review pass.

A closure commit is created only after an independent review has established the exact candidate as CLEAN (or explicitly CLEAN_WITH_WAIVERS under project policy).

## 13.2 Closure commit semantics

A closure commit is metadata-only.

It may record decisions already established by review/evidence, but must not introduce a new blocker-impacting:

- classification;
- attribution;
- waiver;
- severity decision;
- semantic finding decision;
- product contract.

Review/evidence makes the decision. Closure records it.

## 13.3 Required closure fields

Each closed defect record should include:

- defect ID and title;
- final independent verdict;
- implementation starting SHA;
- all semantic implementation/review-fix SHAs attributable to the defect;
- final independently reviewed implementation SHA;
- review record identifier/path if applicable;
- original blocker summary;
- blocker closure summary;
- P0/P1/P2 count at closure;
- exact tests executed and results;
- tests added but not executed;
- compile/build results;
- emulator/device evidence;
- Samsung physical-device line where relevant;
- residual non-blocking risks;
- accepted waivers, only if already independently approved;
- relationships to still-open defects;
- next execution-queue pointer.

## 13.4 Example closure record

```text
Defect: BUG-OUTPUT-01
Status: CLEAN

Implementation start: <sha>
Semantic commits:
  - <sha> ...
  - <sha> ...
Final reviewed implementation: <sha>
Independent review: <review-record>

Open blockers at closure:
  P0: 0
  P1: 0
  P2: 0

Evidence:
  git diff --check: PASS
  focused JVM: PASS
  compileDebugKotlin: PASS
  compileDebugAndroidTestKotlin: PASS
  production wiring: PASS
  Samsung SM-A546E: NOT EXECUTED — DEVICE UNAVAILABLE

Residual non-blockers:
  ...
```

## 13.5 Commit naming

Recommended closure commit concept:

```text
review: close BUG-OUTPUT-01
review: close BUG-PLAYER-01
review: close BUG-QUEUE-03
```

Use repository/project conventions if an established naming scheme is stricter.

## 13.6 Ledger branch isolation

Closure commits belong on `ledger/remediation`, not as semantic parents of implementation commits on `checkpoint/pre-baseline-review`.

Do not merge ledger-only metadata into the implementation chain merely to make the implementation branch “contain” the review.

---

# 14. Evidence and verification gates

## 14.1 Evidence vocabulary

Use exactly and honestly:

- `PASS`
- `FAIL`
- `ATTEMPTED NOT COMPLETED`
- `FAIL BEFORE EXECUTION`
- `ADDED NOT EXECUTED`
- `NOT EXECUTED`
- `SOURCE-LEVEL ONLY`
- `SOURCE-LEVEL FIXED`

Physical Samsung SM-A546E Android 16/API 36 when unavailable:

`NOT EXECUTED — DEVICE UNAVAILABLE`

Never call an unexecuted test PASS.

## 14.2 Initial failure vs later rerun

If a command initially fails and later passes, preserve both facts in the report.

Examples:

```text
compileDebugKotlin:
  initial: FAIL — production compile error in ...
  after correction: PASS
```

or:

```text
instrumentation:
  initial: FAIL BEFORE EXECUTION — emulator unavailable
  rerun: PASS — API 36 emulator
```

Do not rewrite history as if only the final pass happened.

## 14.3 Per-correction minimum gate

Every semantic correction should normally include:

1. `git diff --check`;
2. focused unit tests for the corrected invariant;
3. focused negative tests;
4. compile of affected Kotlin source;
5. affected instrumentation compile where Android tests changed;
6. production-boundary test if the defect is defined at a Room/Worker/filesystem/lifecycle boundary;
7. neighboring regression tests identified in the dossier;
8. fresh self-review of the modified source.

## 14.4 Serial Gradle rule

Use serial Gradle execution when shared Gradle/KSP/emulator/resource state can cause false concurrency failures.

Do not interpret daemon/resource contention or a test-harness timeout as semantic proof either for or against correctness.

## 14.5 Compiler hotspot rule

`DownloadWorker.kt` is historically a Kotlin coroutine/compiler hotspot. For large changes:

- compile early;
- avoid unnecessary nested generic/coroutine complexity;
- distinguish compiler resource/harness failure from semantic failure;
- do not simplify ownership semantics merely to appease compiler complexity without review.

## 14.6 Current toolchain baseline

Unless fresh repository configuration changed it, the historical plan records:

- one app module;
- Gradle 8.13;
- AGP 8.13.2;
- Kotlin 2.3.0;
- KSP 2.3.4;
- JDK 21.

Fresh source/build configuration wins if any version has changed.

---

# 15. Clean policy and cumulative gates

## 15.1 Per-defect CLEAN

CLEAN is determined by the **entire agreed in-scope defect domain at the exact reviewed candidate**, not merely by lines introduced in the current commit. Attribution and blocking status are separate dimensions.

### `CLEAN`

- open in-scope P0 = 0;
- open in-scope P1 = 0;
- open in-scope P2 = 0;
- this includes blockers introduced by the candidate, pre-existing blockers, and blockers merely discovered during the review;
- no accepted/waived P1/P2 unless the project explicitly uses the waiver state below;
- required available execution evidence supports the semantic claim.

### `CLEAN_WITH_WAIVERS`

- no unwaived in-scope P0/P1/P2 blocker remains;
- no P0 may be waived for baseline closure;
- any P1 exception requires an explicit project-owner decision plus independently documented risk and is exceptional rather than routine;
- any P2 waiver requires explicit project-owner approval recorded by independent review;
- a ledger closure commit may record an already approved waiver but may not create one.

### `NOT_CLEAN`

- any unwaived in-scope P0/P1/P2 blocker remains, regardless of attribution; or
- a required available production-boundary verification is missing in a way that prevents the claimed semantic closure.

Absence of a physical device alone does not automatically block closure when equivalent available evidence is sufficient; report it truthfully.

## 15.2 Cross-defect regression gate

A newly implemented defect cannot be considered a valid candidate if it clearly regresses an already independently CLEAN invariant.

The implementation model must repair such regression before moving on when attributable to its change.

## 15.3 Finding A special rule

Finding A remains CLEAN unless fresh actual regression evidence appears.

Do not repeatedly re-litigate its historical findings without a current regression trigger, but run focused regressions when later changes touch its execution/History/cancellation authority surfaces.

## 15.4 Finding B and F1 gates

Finding B must not start before BUG-OUTPUT-01 independent CLEAN under the current project decision. This is a dependency gate, not a global freeze: unrelated actionable defects may proceed while BUG-OUTPUT-01 review is pending.

After Finding B becomes independently CLEAN, perform the mandatory full current-source F1 / `BUG-BACKUP-01` close-out review before any downstream backup/restore defect relies on F1 as closed. While that independent close-out is pending, the runner continues unrelated actionable defects; only work that semantically depends on F1 closure remains blocked.

If an explicit independent-review/project decision changes this ordering, record that decision rather than silently ignoring the gate.

---

# 16. Priority queue after BUG-OUTPUT-01

This queue is intentionally more adaptive than the historical F1→F22 numerical order. Hard dependencies override ordinal preference; fresh independent P0/P1 review findings override planned work.

## 16.1 Immediate dependency-aware sequence

The ordinal below expresses preferred priority **when an item is actionable**. If an item is blocked awaiting independent review, skip temporarily to the highest-priority unrelated actionable item and return as soon as the gate opens or a review blocker arrives.

1. `BUG-OUTPUT-01` — finish exact output confinement/provenance and publish a candidate.
2. BUG-OUTPUT independent review/closure; while pending, Finding B is blocked but unrelated work may advance.
3. Finding B / remaining `BUG-BACKUP-01` source-less command authority closure, immediately when BUG-OUTPUT becomes independently CLEAN.
4. Finding B independent review; while pending, unrelated work may advance.
5. Full current-source F1 / `BUG-BACKUP-01` close-out review and ledger closure immediately when Finding B becomes independently CLEAN.
6. Current Player timeline/index crash P1.
7. Crash evidence preservation.
8. MainActivity startup/lifecycle root cause if evidence is sufficient.
9. Link input/share extraction contract.
10. Canonical media/source identity.
11. History title-only destructive duplicate correction.
12. `BUG-QUEUE-03` scheduler acceptance/recovery.
13. Preset/LayoutParams crash.
14. Quality degradation semantics only after product decision.
15. Worker/foreground anomaly only after synchronized evidence.

